"A drawn circle and a drawn ball have the same outline. What makes one of them look round?": "80eebd087018",
"A low lamp casts a long shadow.": "e2aae7fc306f",
"Artists have names for the parts. The highlight is the brightest spot, where a shiny surface sends the lamp's light straight to your eye. The light side shades into the halftone, the middle grays. Then comes the core shadow, a soft dark band that begins where the direct light ends. Beyond it the shadow side is often a little lighter again. That is reflected light, which bounces up from the table onto the ball.": "5948f141f34e",
"Careful shading makes the circle look like a ball.": "feabe748bec3",
"Draw a circle on a sheet of paper. It could be a coin, a plate, or a hole. Nothing in the outline tells you which. Now make one side of the circle light and let it grow darker little by little toward the other side, and add a dark patch on the ground beside it. Suddenly the circle is a ball. The outline did not change. What you added is shading, and shading is how a flat drawing shows that a thing is round.": "2dc5695d6834",
"Draw a circle on a sheet of paper. It could be a coin, a plate, or a hole. Nothing in the outline tells you which. Now make one side of the circle light and let it grow darker little by little toward the other side, and add a dark patch on the ground beside it. Suddenly the circle is a ball. The outline did not change. What you added is shading, and shading is how a flat drawing shows that a thing is round. Shading copies what light does. Put a ball on a table and switch on one lamp. The part of the ball that faces the lamp gets the most light. As the surface curves away, the same light is spread over more of it, so it looks darker. Where the surface turns its back on the lamp, no direct light arrives at all. Every gray on the ball depends on one thing: how squarely that spot faces the light. Artists have names for the parts. The highlight is the brightest spot, where a shiny surface sends the lamp's light straight to your eye. The light side shades into the halftone, the middle grays. Then comes the core shadow, a soft dark band that begins where the direct light ends. Beyond it the shadow side is often a little lighter again. That is reflected light, which bounces up from the table onto the ball. The ball also blocks light from reaching the table, and that makes a cast shadow. A cast shadow always points away from the lamp. Raise the lamp and the shadow gets shorter. Lower the lamp and it stretches out, the way your own shadow does late in the afternoon. A small lamp gives a shadow with a crisp edge. The wider the light source, the more blurred the shadow becomes. You do not need hundreds of grays to draw a ball. How light or dark a color is, is called its value, and a value scale is a row of even steps from white to black. In the Munsell system, value runs from 0 for black to 10 for white. In 1907 the teacher Denman Ross described a scale of nine values. With only five steps of pencil gray, placed in the right places, a circle already looks round. Painters have used light and shade this way for centuries. The Italian word chiaroscuro means light-dark, and it describes clear contrasts of tone that suggest the volume of the things in a painting. Leonardo da Vinci and Caravaggio are famous for it. You can see the same thing in the night sky. The Sun always lights half of the Moon, like one lamp shining on one ball, and what we call the phases are our changing view of that sunlit half.": "c20268296023",
